<title>USA: U.S. June construction spending fell 1.1 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. June construction spending fell 1.1 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-08-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Commerce Department seasonally adjusted construction spending, with comparisons.</p>
<p> PERCENT CHANGE:    June     May--(Prev)   Apr-(Prev) Jun97/96</p>
<p> Total Spending     -1.1     0.3   -1.8    0.4    0.3	4.1</p>
<p> ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:		    Jun'96</p>
<p> Total Spending    591.5   597.9  585.0  596.2  595.7    568.2</p>
<p> Private Spending  454.8   462.0  453.7  456.9  456.0    438.6</p>
<p> -Residential	256.4   259.5  253.6  260.0  254.2    250.2</p>
<p> --New Housing     181.8   185.2  183.3  185.3  184.2    182.4</p>
<p> ---Single-Family  159.3   161.0  159.8  162.5  161.6    161.1</p>
<p> ---Multi-Family    22.5    24.2   23.5   22.8   22.6     21.3</p>
<p>ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:</p>
<p> -			June     May--(Prev)   Apr-(Prev)   Jun'96</p>
<p> -Nonresidential   158.9   163.4  160.4  156.5  161.4    150.4</p>
<p> --Industrial	 31.5    31.1   31.4   29.4   30.9     32.0</p>
<p> --Office	     30.7    29.8   29.7   28.7   29.8     29.8</p>
<p> Public Spending   136.7   135.8  131.3  139.3  139.6    129.6</p>
<p> PERCENT CHANGE:    June     May--(Prev)   Apr-(Prev) Jun97/96</p>
<p> Private Spending   -1.6     1.1   -0.5    0.9    0.7	3.7</p>
<p> Public Spending     0.7    -2.5   -5.9   -1.3   -1.1	5.5</p>
<p>Construction spending in 1992 dollars, which discounts the effects of inflation:</p>
<p> PERCENT CHANGE:    June     May--(Prev)   Apr--(Prev)</p>
<p>  Total Spending    -1.4    -0.2   -2.2	    UNCH</p>
<p>ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:</p>
<p>  Total Spending   499.3   506.2  495.9  507.1  507.2</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast overall spending on construction would rise 1.1 percent in June instead of fall.  </p>
<p>The drop in June construction spending was the largest in six months since a 1.2 percent decline in December last year, the department said.</p>
<p>The department sharply revised its previously published estimate of May construction spending to show a 0.3 percent increase to a seasonally adjusted annual rate of $597.9 billion instead of a sharp 1.8 percent drop to a $585 billion rate.</p>
</text>
